# DNS over HTTPS — RFC 8484, served by the API listener (p3-05)
#
# `/dns-query` is a DNS front door, not an admin endpoint: it is the one route
# outside `/api/v1/` and it carries no authentication (a DNS client cannot
# present a bearer key or a cookie — SECURITY.md §API access). It answers only
# when `[dns.listen] doh_enabled = true` (the default); disabled, the path falls
# through to the dashboard shell and never returns application/dns-message.
#
# Both forms return 200 with Content-Type: application/dns-message and
# Cache-Control: no-store. The verdict is the same one UDP/53 would give the
# same client (the peer address of the HTTPS connection).

@base = {{host}}

### GET form (RFC 8484 §4.1): `dns` is the wire-format query, base64url without
### padding. This is the RFC's own example — an A query for www.example.com
### with id 0.
GET {{base}}/dns-query?dns=AAABAAABAAAAAAAAA3d3dwdleGFtcGxlA2NvbQAAAQAB
Accept: application/dns-message

### POST form (RFC 8484 §4.1): the wire-format query as the body. The body
### file is the same 33-byte query the GET above carries.
POST {{base}}/dns-query
Content-Type: application/dns-message
Accept: application/dns-message

< ./dns-query.bin

### A padded `dns` value is rejected (400) — the RFC forbids padding, and the
### rejection is plain text, never application/dns-message.
GET {{base}}/dns-query?dns=AAABAAABAAAAAAAAA3d3dwdleGFtcGxlA2NvbQAAAQAB==

### The wrong media type is rejected (415).
POST {{base}}/dns-query
Content-Type: application/json

{"not": "dns"}
